									</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/ocuin" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(6104981); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=6104981&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1609250027" alt="OCuin" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=6104981)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/ocuin" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(6104981); return false;" >OCuin</a></span> <span class="odate time_1609248895 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">29 Dec 2020 13:34</span>
																	<br/>
						in discussion <a href="/forum/c-89000/help:ideas-critique">Writing Help / Help: Ideas Critique</a> &raquo;
						<a href="/forum/t-13948232/hackerman-b">Hackerman B)</a>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4856595">
									

<p>Summoned by PM. My apologies for the delay.</p>
<p>This feels rather generic, and it's not the strongest start when you start by saying the anomaly is pretty boring. This feels like an anomalous humanoid with no real interesting traits - why spoiling the plot of something? What purpose does that serve in terms of the story and why does it matter? It feels very arbitrary and I don't see why it's here. Similarly, why is he a proficient hacker? What story purpose does this serve? There's no clear connection between the anomaly and the story here, which is a fairly big issue in a storytelling format built around a central anomaly that serves as both premise and main character.</p>
<p>The narrative here isn't nearly detailed enough to give us a clear sense of the quality of this idea or of what will happen in the article. We need a clearer sense of the actual events here - what information will be told to the reader and how, and why it's important. To greenlight, reviewers need confidence in the story above anything else, and to have that we need to know what actually happens.</p>
<p>Finally, I'm not sure why this is a format-screw. It does seem to serve any element of the idea you've described, nor does it really fit with the idea in my opinion. If you want to tell a story in first-person, you might be better writing this as a tale, but either way I'm not sure why this is a format-screw and not just a standard article. At least in my opinion, a format screw needs to be serving the story with its own format - just doing it for the sake of it usually feels a bit hollow.</p>

							</div>

								
					
