<p>Summoned by PM. My apologies for the delay.</p>
<p>Like <span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/cybersqyd" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(5336470); return false;"><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=5336470&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1609246492" alt="cybersqyd" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=5336470)" /></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/cybersqyd" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(5336470); return false;">cybersqyd</a></span>, I like the core of this. An entity formed of collective inhabitation of human minds that asks for increasingly severe demands of the Foundation is a cool premise and one with some interesting possibilities.</p>
<p>My main issue is that the narrative you describe right now feels like it lacks a clear escalation towards a conclusion. You've mentioned the entity asks for increasingly extreme demands, but I don't know what they are so it's difficult to have a full handle on what the escalation would look like here. Additionally, there's no ending here really. Where does the story end and why? How does that fit with the rest of the story? I think the premise here is cool but it needs to be capped with a satisfying conclusion. What is that conclusion?</p>
<p>I'd also second sqyd's comments on the TV show angle - it feels like an aside that doesn't fully fit with the rest of the narrative. It's tonally too different and doesn't fit well enough with the core conflict, I think.</p>

