<p>Hi, thank you for your critique!</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;My&nbsp;response.</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;Close&nbsp;response.</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>I’m not sure how to answer those questions and it’s mostly because I struggle with expressing the thoughts in my head. I have the answers and they make sense to me but writing them down is hard. But I’m going to try.</p>
<p>The whole piece is about the realisation that there’s an SCP that’s made fixing climate change kinda moot, it’s added it’s own irreversible and irrecoverable damage to the climate and that the world is kinda fucked. That’s what I want the reader to come away with.</p>
<p>I don’t know how I can make a character someone can relate to in this kind of piece. I thought I had at least on some small level. This one I really don’t know how to fix.</p>
<p>I want this to say a lot of things, like “hi I am a new writer but I want to be a part of this amazing community” or “this is something I have put time and effort and love into.” It’s probably not the right answer to that question but it’s the answer that feels right to me. This is my first SCP, when I’m gone this will remain as my legacy. I want this to be something people can point to and say “yep, that guy did that. It’s okay I guess.” That’s what I want it to say.</p>
<p>But again that’s not the answer you probably want, so let me try again. I love this SCP because it has potential. I make several references to another function of the object, which is functionally identical to what is described in the article except instead of turning oxygen into helium it turns it into carbon dioxide or water vapour, with the complexity going up the higher the input number. I want it to say there’s more to this, to make people think and imagine.</p>
<p>I did kinda stupidly think that maybe this could’ve been something that other people could collaborate on, creating and adding things to a list of what this SCP could make. But fears of it being compared to other open collaboration SCPs killed that idea. The testing log is the remnant of that I suppose, turned into an attempt to channel a narrative that admittedly has failed. I do understand that it’s a very dry narrative, and ditching the logs has been advised before. But I honestly don’t know how else to channel and describe a narrative through this kind of fiction, I’ve never written anything like an SCP article before so it’s all foreign to me.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

