<p><strong>Seeking Greenlights:</strong> No, I already have pages up</p>
<p><strong>Page Type:</strong> GoI Format</p>
<p><strong>Elevator Pitch:</strong> The people who adopt Scribbles always seem to have magic-related issues. When his messages get decoded, they find out he was spying on them for an avian invasion all along.</p>
<p><strong>Central Narrative:</strong> Scribbles the cedar waxwing can communicate by creating and arranging sticks on the ground into words. Nobody knows where the sticks he produces come from and he never creates sticks on camera. Because he can communicate, he chooses which people adopt him &#8212; all magic users from Three Portlands.</p>
<p>The first person who adopts him, Maria, observes that he keeps pecking lines into a certain stick, which he hides when he notices a human looking. When she comes home to find her magical protection system disabled with beak marks in its components, she returns him.</p>
<p>The second owner, Robert, notices that he's pecking dots and lines in a regular pattern, almost like words. He comes home one day to find ten waxwings attempting to peck through the door to his room full of magical books. Nine of them depart when he enters. Scribbles uses his sticks to ask to be taken back to Wilson's.</p>
<p>The third owner, Keith, grabs Scribbles' strangely pecked stick while Scribbles is engrossed in a magic book and sends it to his friend at Anderson Robotics who specializes in cryptography.</p>
<p>The decoded message shows that Scribbles has been spying on humans, planning an invasion of the city (and later humanity) with his group of waxwings. The stick records his observations and is meant to be passed onto other waxwings when the right moment arrives. He's been trying to access magical areas and study magic as a way of counteracting humans' strength and technology.</p>
<p><strong>Hook/Attention-Grabber:</strong> A cedar waxwing conspiracy sending a spy into Wilson's.</p>
<p><strong>Additional Notes:</strong> I'm trying to decide whether this concept or my <a href="http://www.scpwiki.com/forum/t-13947517/critter-profile:emma-and-cream#post-4849861">other</a> Wilson's concept is better.</p>

