<p>I'm&#8230;kinda torn on this as a whole. There's some really interesting elements, and the actual setting and concept seems pretty cool; but it feels a little like you don't really do anything interesting with it: there's no juicy narrative to really explore why this is interesting, and it kinda feels like this just builds up to the mystery of who the director is in an unsatisfying way?</p>
<p>Or well, rather, it feels like this has two narrative threads: the exploration of the patients and the exploration of the hospital. The first feels like a very kinda, incohesive exploration of mental illness and the effects of the hospital; whereas the second really lets you just, explore a strange building and I guess my question is really: what's the focus here? What's the overall narrative arc of this piece?</p>
<p>I share <span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/ethanhanson" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(4465845); return false;"><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=4465845&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1609238977" alt="EthanHanson" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=4465845)" /></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/ethanhanson" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(4465845); return false;">EthanHanson</a></span>'s concerns about the Foundation using this to store anomalies: it feels like it's something plausible if they're a lot more confident about what this place is like; but it feels to me that the more interesting thing is exploring the foundation just having found it and like, really allowing the exploration of the place to let this shine.</p>

							</div>

								
					
